{=============================================================================
  Two rigid tanks connected by a valve: change of quality of R12
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0048
 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Two rigid tanks A and B of 200 L each are connected by a valve, as in the
  original problem statement. Tank A holds R12 at 25 C with, in volume, 10 %
  of liquid and 90 % of vapour; tank B is empty. The valve is opened and
  saturated vapour escapes from A until the pressure of B reaches that of A;
  the valve is then closed again. The operation is slow, so the temperature
  stays at 25 C during the whole process. By how much has the quality in tank A
  changed at the end of the process?

  The model first computes the initial quality of tank A from its initial
  liquid and vapour volumes. Since the operation is slow and isothermal, the
  pressure stays at the saturation pressure of 25 C: liquid evaporates and
  fills tank B. The distribution of the refrigerant between liquid and vapour
  in the whole volume is obtained from two equations (total volume, total
  mass) for two unknowns (Vol_l, Vol_v), which are then linked to the masses
  through the specific volumes at saturation. The final quality of tank A
  follows from the vapour volume left in A, Vol_v - Vol_B.

  Inputs : Vol_A, Vol_B, T_A, f_m_l_A, f_m_v_A
  Outputs: x_A_i, x_A_f, Deltax_A (change of quality in A, in percentage
           points), m_l_A_f, m_v_A_f, m_A_f, m_B_f
  Units  : SI - temperatures in C, pressures in Pa, energies in J
  Source : ULiege course Thermodynamique appliquee (MECA0002), repetition
           session 1, exercise 4 (2022-2023) - see README.md
=============================================================================}

"!Problem statement (English paraphrase of the original French statement)"
"Two tanks A and B are connected by a valve. Each tank has a volume of 200 L. Tank A contains R12 at 25 C and, in
 volume, 10 % of liquid and 90 % of vapour. Tank B is empty. The valve is then opened and saturated vapour escapes
 from A until the pressure of B reaches that of A. The valve is then closed again. This operation takes place
 slowly, so that the temperatures remain at 25 C during its whole duration. By how much has the quality in tank A
 changed as a result of the process?"

"!Inputs"
Vol_A = 200e-3 [m^3]  "volume of tank A [m^3]"
Vol_B = 200e-3 [m^3]  "volume of tank B [m^3]"
T_A = 25.0 [C]  "temperature of the process, kept at 25 C during the whole operation [C] (298.15 K in the original)"
f_m_l_A = 0.1  "initial liquid fraction of the volume of tank A [-] (a volume fraction in the original, as in its comment and in its use)"
f_m_v_A = 0.9  "initial vapour fraction of the volume of tank A [-] (a volume fraction in the original, as in its comment and in its use)"

"!Initial quality of the refrigerant in tank A"
Vol_l_A = f_m_l_A*Vol_A  "volume occupied by the liquid in A [m^3]"
Vol_v_A = f_m_v_A*Vol_A  "volume occupied by the vapour in A [m^3]"
v_l = volume(R12,T=T_A,x=0)  "specific volume of saturated liquid R12 at T_A [m^3/kg]"
v_v = volume(R12,T=T_A,x=1)  "specific volume of saturated vapour R12 at T_A [m^3/kg]"
m_l_A = Vol_l_A/v_l  "mass of liquid [kg]"
m_v_A = Vol_v_A/v_v  "mass of vapour [kg]"
m_tot = m_l_A+m_v_A  "total mass of refrigerant [kg]"
x_A_i = m_v_A/m_tot  "initial quality of the refrigerant in A [-]"

"!Distribution of the refrigerant in each tank at the end of the operation"
"The pressure is allowed to equilibrate before the valve is closed, so the pressure stays equal to the saturation
 pressure at 25 C. Liquid is therefore converted into vapour to fill tank B."
Vol_tot = Vol_A+Vol_B  "the total volume is the sum of the two tank volumes [m^3]"
Vol_tot = Vol_l+Vol_v  "the total volume contains an unknown quantity of liquid and an unknown quantity of vapour [m^3]"
m_tot = m_l+m_v  "the total mass is conserved and splits into vapour and liquid in unknown proportions [kg]"
"The system of 2 equations for the 2 unknowns above gives the distribution, in mass and in volume, of the refrigerant
 in the global volume"

"!Links between volume and mass: the specific volume, by definition"
Vol_l = m_l*v_l  "volume occupied by the liquid of the whole set [m^3]"
Vol_v = m_v*v_v  "volume occupied by the vapour of the whole set [m^3]"

"!Final state of tank A"
"Only vapour can pass from A to B. Immediately after the valve is closed, tank B holds only vapour and all the
 liquid stays in A"
Vol_v_A_f = Vol_v-Vol_B  "volume of vapour in A at the end of the process [m^3]"
dVol_A = Vol_v_A_f+Vol_l-Vol_A  "check on the total volume of A [m^3]"
m_l_A_f = Vol_l/v_l  "mass of liquid in A in the final state [kg]"
m_v_A_f = Vol_v_A_f/v_v  "mass of vapour in A in the final state [kg]"
m_A_f = m_l_A_f+m_v_A_f  "total mass of refrigerant in A in the final state [kg]"
x_A_f = m_v_A_f/m_A_f  "final quality of the refrigerant in A [-]"
m_B_f = Vol_B/v_v  "mass of refrigerant in B [kg]"
dm_tot = m_A_f+m_B_f-m_tot  "check on the total mass of refrigerant in A and B [kg]"
Deltax_A = 100*(x_A_f-x_A_i)  "variation of the quality in A during the process [percentage points], dimensionless"